Fleet Systems: Lifecycles & Ownership

TC1030.302 Object-Oriented Programming
Sebastian Villegas Olaya · A01714719

Build & run

g++ -std=c++14 main.cpp -o fleet
./fleet

I also built it with -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic and it compiles with no warnings.

Part 1: Relationships and ownership

For the ships themselves, the Fleet owns them with
std::vector<std::unique_ptr<Spacecraft>>. I went with unique_ptr instead of a
raw pointer so I don't have to delete anything by hand, and instead of
shared_ptr because each ship has exactly one owner (the Fleet), so paying for
reference counting wouldn't buy anything.

All data members are private. The only exception is name_ and powerDraw_ in
Module, which are protected because Engine and Shield read them directly.

Part 2: Construction and destruction order

When one ship (Aurora-01) is created and then destroyed, the lines come out in
this order.

Construction:

[ctor] Entity      Aurora-01     (base built first)
[ctor]  FuelTank   (fuel=80)     (member 1)
[ctor]  Telemetry  (...)         (member 2)
[ctor] Spacecraft  Aurora-01     (derived body last)

Destruction is the reverse:

[dtor] Spacecraft  Aurora-01     (derived body first)
[dtor]  Telemetry  (...)         (member 2 first, reverse order)
[dtor]  FuelTank                 (member 1 next)
[dtor] Entity      Aurora-01     (base last)

The base is built first and destroyed last, and members are destroyed in the
reverse of how they were declared. That's why FuelTank comes before Telemetry on
the way up and after it on the way down. The Module isn't in this list because
the ship doesn't own it (it's aggregated through a non-owning pointer), so it's
built and destroyed on its own.

The Module hierarchy behaves the same way. Building an Engine prints
[ctor] Module then [ctor] Engine, and deleting it through a
unique_ptr<Module> prints [dtor] Engine then [dtor] Module. That reverse
order is the whole reason the base destructor has to be virtual.

Virtual destructor

Entity and Module both have a virtual destructor. Ships and modules get
deleted through a base pointer (unique_ptr<Spacecraft>, Module*). If the base
destructor weren't virtual, delete basePtr would run only the base destructor
and the derived part would leak. Making it virtual means the derived destructor
runs first and then the base. There's a comment inside the Module class showing
the broken version before I fixed it.

Slicing

Slicing is when you copy a derived object into a base-typed slot by value, like
std::vector<Module> bay; bay.push_back(Engine(...)). Only the Module part
gets copied and the Engine part is lost, so activate() would call the base
version. I avoid it by never storing these objects by value: modules are held as
Module* and ships as unique_ptr<Spacecraft>, so virtual dispatch still works.

Parts 3 to 5

Part 3 (Rule of 5). CargoHold owns a raw int* array, so I had to write all
five special members myself: destructor, copy constructor (deep copy), copy
assignment (with a self-assignment check and releasing the old buffer), and move
constructor and move assignment (both noexcept, stealing the pointer and
nulling the source). main runs all five plus a self-assignment, and prints the
buffer addresses so you can see the copy is independent and nothing gets freed
twice.

Part 4 (Rule of 0). ModernCargoHold does the same job with a
std::vector<int> and zero special members, because the vector already handles
copy, move and destruction correctly. The Fleet's
vector<unique_ptr<Spacecraft>> is the other half of this: removing one ship
frees only that ship and leaves the rest of the fleet alone.

Part 5 (exceptions and RAII). dock() throws a custom DockingException
(from std::runtime_error) when there isn't enough fuel. Right before the check
it grabs a DockingClamp with make_unique, so if the throw happens the clamp
still gets released during stack unwinding and nothing leaks. The exception is
caught in the simulation loop so the sim keeps running.
